# Restricts this resolver to exactly one client: sieve's Pi-hole. Unbound
# matches access-control rules by longest-prefix-match, not first- or
# last-line-wins — a /32 here is maximally specific and always takes
# precedence for this one address over whatever broader access-control
# rules klutchell/unbound's own default config ships with, regardless of
# load order. That property is why a single additive line here is enough,
# without needing to know or override the base image's full default
# config — confirmed via Unbound's own documented matching behavior before
# relying on it, not guessed.
#
# An open recursive resolver is a real, well-known abuse vector (DNS
# amplification attacks) — this line is the only thing standing between
# "internal resolver for sieve" and "open resolver reachable by anyone who
# can route to silo." Backlog: verify for real after bring-up —
# `dig @${SILO_LAN_IP} example.com` from any LAN device that ISN'T sieve
# should come back REFUSED or time out, not answer.
server:
  access-control: ${SIEVE_LAN_IP}/32 allow
